WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.320
Mate two yellow mice as often as you like, and you will never get a yellow mouse

2
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.500
that breeds true. That looked like a failure of Mendel's rules.

3
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:19.480
It was not. Segregation and independent assortment never stop working.

4
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.820
What changes is the map from genotype to phenotype.

5
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:30.380
Cross red snapdragons with white ones, and every F1 flower is pink.

6
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.160
This is incomplete dominance.

7
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:40.800
Self the pink plants, and red and white come back, at one red, two pink, one white.

8
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:45.320
Nothing blended, because a pink plant is a heterozygote.

9
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:53.240
In Mendel's peas the one to two to one was hidden, but here the colours show it plainly.

10
00:00:53.583 --> 00:00:59.243
Now back to the yellow mouse, whose yellow allele is dominant for coat colour but

11
00:00:59.243 --> 00:01:00.523
recessive for survival.

12
00:01:01.343 --> 00:01:06.923
A mouse with two copies dies before implantation, so one quarter of the conceptions

13
00:01:06.923 --> 00:01:13.863
die. The survivors are two yellow to one black, so twelve live pups should be eight

14
00:01:13.863 --> 00:01:15.223
yellow and four black.

15
00:01:16.183 --> 00:01:19.363
Every live yellow mouse is therefore a heterozygote.

16
00:01:19.863 --> 00:01:21.623
That is why none breeds true.

17
00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:28.887
When two genes act on one trait, a genotype at one can hide the other, which is

18
00:01:28.887 --> 00:01:35.447
called epistasis. In Labrador retrievers, a dominant B makes black pigment, and its

19
00:01:35.447 --> 00:01:38.027
recessive form makes brown, called chocolate.

20
00:01:39.047 --> 00:01:44.647
Cross two black dogs heterozygous at both genes, and the 16 boxes sort into the

21
00:01:44.647 --> 00:01:45.927
usual four classes.

22
00:01:46.527 --> 00:01:51.487
But a dog with two copies of the recessive form of E is yellow, whatever its B

23
00:01:51.487 --> 00:01:58.967
genotype. So the last two classes merge, and the ratio is 9 black, 3 chocolate, 4 yellow.

24
00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:03.067
Other interactions regroup the same 16 boxes.

25
00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:09.427
If both genes need a dominant allele to make colour, the ratio is 9 to 7.

26
00:02:10.127 --> 00:02:16.547
If a dominant allele at one gene hides the other, the ratio is 12 to 3 to 1.

27
00:02:17.287 --> 00:02:22.607
If a dominant allele at either gene is enough, the ratio is 15 to 1.

28
00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:26.927
In every case, only the labels on the boxes change.

29
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:31.140
The boxes never changed, only their labels.

30
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:35.080
One allele hides the other, and you see three to one.

31
00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.060
Neither hides, you see one to two to one.

32
00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.620
and if one class dies, you see two to one.

33
00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:50.980
Keep this rule. Segregation never stops working, and only the map from genotype to phenotype changes.
